tive prime bases with rational exponents; no sums and no complex branch choices.","argument":"If two products are equal, divide and raise to a common positive multiple N of every exponent denominator. Moving negative powers to the other side gives equality of positive integers with prime factorizations. Unique prime factorization forces every N*(alpha_p-beta_p)=0, hence alpha_p=beta_p. Conversely identical exponents give identical positive products.","source_refs":["9e03168cabf04e3887245d93c75f1f9d0034a53bead61e5d8b5a5e0315b259e8"],"source_pointer":"/generative_registry/0"},{"id":"ALG-G","status":"derived from native generators","statement":"G=<R,L>={2^a3^b:a,b in Z}.","argument":"R,L have (nu2,nu3) columns(1,-1),(-2,1), determinant-1. Thus they span the complete integer lattice. Explicitly2=R^-1 L^-1 and3=R^-2 L^-1.","source_refs":["9e03168cabf04e3887245d93c75f1f9d0034a53bead61e5d8b5a5e0315b259e8"],"source_pointer":"/generative_registry/0/proved_relations/0"},{"id":"ALG-H","status":"derived from native root extension","statement":"H=<G,q>={2^(a/12)3^b:a,b in Z}, H/G is C12.","argument":"Adding q adds(1/12,0) to the G lattice. Modulo G only a mod12 survives. Distinct residues0..11 cannot collapse because faithfulness would require their prime2exponent difference to be an integer.","source_refs":["9e03168cabf04e3887245d93c75f1f9d0034a53bead61e5d8b5a5e0315b259e8"],"source_pointer":"/generative_registry/0/proved_relations/2"},{"id":"ALG-OCTAVE","status":"derived quotient distinction","statement":"H/<2> is C12 x Z and is infinite.","argument":"The homomorphism2^(a/12)3^b -> (a mod12,b) is surjective, with kernel{2^m:m in Z}. The second coordinate remains an arbitrary integer. This quotient differs from H/G, which also discards all integer prime3powers.","source_refs":["9e03168cabf04e3887245d93c75f1f9d0034a53bead61e5d8b5a5e0315b259e8"],"source_pointer":"/generative_registry/0"},{"id":"ALG-FIFTH","status":"new connected consequence of retained native operators","statement